[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r6
diagnosis_text:
The outage is not on R7. The failing component is the OSPF daemon on R6 (ospfd/FRR), which is R7’s only OSPF adjacency upstream. Evidence:
- On R6, ospfd is not running and the FRR service is failed (systemctl: “Active: failed”; “ospfd is not running”). The FRR log records: “Client ‘ospf’ encountered an error and is shutting down … 64 ospf routes removed from the rib,” confirming an OSPF process crash and route withdrawal on R6.
- R7’s OSPF is up and correctly configured to advertise its loopbacks (17.x), the R6–R7 link (67.0.0.0/8), and its LAN (70.0.0.0/8). R7 shows an active OSPF process but zero neighbors, which is expected if its only peer (R6) went down.
- On R1, “show ip route 67.0.0.0/8” and “70.0.0.0/8” both return “% Network not in table,” matching the report that R7-region routes vanished networkwide once R6 withdrew LSAs.

Mechanism: With R6’s ospfd down, R7 lost its sole adjacency and its LSAs (17.x loopbacks, 67/8, 70/8) stopped being flooded into Area 1/backbone. Zebra on R6 flushed OSPF-learned entries, causing upstream ABRs and R1 to drop those prefixes.

Localization: Device R6, OSPF daemon (ospfd). The R6–R7 link itself remains up as a connected network on both sides.

Root cause: OSPF daemon crash on R6 caused LSA withdrawal from the network.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR/ospfd on R6 (and enable watchfrr), verify /etc/frr/frr.conf and /etc/frr/daemons, and check logs/core for crash cause. Once ospfd on R6 is restored, R7’s adjacencies and routes (17.x, 67/8, 70/8) should reappear on R1. 
=== END ===